ipv4: free inet_opt and ireq_opt after an RCU grace period

Backport of upstream e3f33b0a1d89 ("ipv4: free inet_opt and ireq_opt
after an RCU grace period"), which is CVE-2026-98432 and is queued for
6.12.113 as 11a03ce3c4a6.  Our tree is 6.12.112, so we carry it here.

tcp_v4_syn_recv_sock() hands ownership of ireq->ireq_opt to the child
socket (newinet->inet_opt) without copying it.  A retransmitted SYN for
the same request socket can be processed on another CPU at the same
time and send a SYNACK from tcp_check_req(); tcp_v4_send_synack() and
inet_csk_route_req() read ireq->ireq_opt under rcu_read_lock() only,
and ip_build_and_send_pkt() / ip_options_build() then read opt->optlen.

Since 079096f103fa ("tcp/dccp: install syn_recv requests into ehash
table") request sockets are processed without the listener lock, so
nothing stops the child socket from being freed while the SYNACK is
still being built.  TCP child sockets do not have SOCK_RCU_FREE, and
inet_sock_destruct() released inet_opt with a plain kfree() -> UAF in
ip_options_build().

The same race exists for request-socket migration
(net.ipv4.tcp_migrate_req=1, or a BPF_SK_REUSEPORT_SELECT_OR_MIGRATE
program): reqsk_timer_handler() clones the request socket with
inet_reqsk_clone() so the old socket and the clone share ireq_opt, and
reqsk_migrate_reset() clears the pointer in the old one, while another
CPU holding a reference on the old socket may still be using the
options.  tcp_v4_reqsk_destructor() also used a plain kfree().

All readers are already RCU-protected and the other paths that replace
inet_opt (do_ip_setsockopt(), cipso_v4_sock_setattr(), ...) already use
kfree_rcu(), so switch these two destructors over as well.  kfree_rcu()
is NULL-safe, and struct ip_options_rcu carries the struct rcu_head as
its first member, so no other change is needed.

Reachable on this router from the WAN side by a peer that opens TCP
connections with IP options and races a SYN retransmission against the
handshake; low probability per attempt, but it is a remotely triggerable
use-after-free, so it is worth the two lines.

--- a/net/ipv4/af_inet.c
+++ b/net/ipv4/af_inet.c
@@ -155,7 +155,7 @@
 	WARN_ON_ONCE(sk->sk_wmem_queued);
 	WARN_ON_ONCE(sk_forward_alloc_get(sk));
 
-	kfree(rcu_dereference_protected(inet->inet_opt, 1));
+	kfree_rcu(rcu_dereference_protected(inet->inet_opt, 1), rcu);
 	dst_release(rcu_dereference_protected(sk->sk_dst_cache, 1));
 	dst_release(rcu_dereference_protected(sk->sk_rx_dst, 1));
 }
--- a/net/ipv4/tcp_ipv4.c
+++ b/net/ipv4/tcp_ipv4.c
@@ -1219,7 +1219,7 @@
  */
 static void tcp_v4_reqsk_destructor(struct request_sock *req)
 {
-	kfree(rcu_dereference_protected(inet_rsk(req)->ireq_opt, 1));
+	kfree_rcu(rcu_dereference_protected(inet_rsk(req)->ireq_opt, 1), rcu);
 }
 
